-`--style runs` (default) merges adjacent dots into single capsule strokes; `--style dots` draws every
-dot separately. Both carry identical data. `--fg/--bg` recolour the symbol (the decoder also accepts
-light-on-dark and mirrored images).
+`--style runs` (default) merges neighbouring cells of the **same level** into capsule strokes; `--style dots`
+draws every cell separately. Both carry identical data. `--fg/--bg` recolour the symbol (the decoder also
+accepts light-on-dark and mirrored images).
 
-## Logos in the centre
+## The symbol
 
-`--logo <file>` reserves a clear circular zone in the middle of the symbol and puts the logo there.
-The zone is *not* part of the data, so a logo costs no error-correction budget (unlike a QR logo, which
-overwrites modules). The finder becomes two rings around the zone, so logo symbols are separate versions:
-
-| `--logo-size` | Zone radius | Zone diameter vs symbol width | Versions (32 B / 80 B payload) |
-|---|---|---|---|
-| small | 4.0 units | ~16% | 3 / 4 |
-| medium (default) | 5.5 | ~20% | 5 / 6 |
-| large | 7.0 | ~25% | 7 / 8 |
-
-Larger zones barely enlarge the symbol (outer rings are longer, so fewer rings are needed).
-
-- **Fit:** by default the smallest circle enclosing the logo's *visible ink* is scaled to fill 97% of the zone,
-  so round seals fill it, off-centre artwork is re-centred, and rectangular logos fit with nothing clipped.
-  `--logo-fit viewbox` fits the whole image rectangle instead; `--logo-scale 0.8` shrinks, `1.2` enlarges.
-- **Backdrop:** `--logo-bg #rrggbb` sets the colour behind the logo (default: the symbol background). A logo
-  with light strokes (e.g. `#CECDC3`) vanishes on a light symbol; use a dark theme (`--bg #100F0F --fg #CECDC3`)
-  or a dark backdrop (`--logo-bg #100F0F`).
-- **SVG output** embeds an SVG logo as live vector (clipped to the zone); PNG/JPEG logos are embedded as data URIs.
-- **PNG output with an SVG logo** uses a built-in mini rasterizer (`internal/svgmini`) because the Go standard
-  library cannot render SVG. It draws filled shapes: path (all commands incl. curves and arcs), rect, circle,
-  ellipse, polygon, polyline, groups, transforms, fill / fill-rule / opacity. It is checked against `cairosvg`
-  on a real logo (mean difference 0.05/255). Anything it cannot draw (strokes, gradients, text, CSS `<style>`,
-  `<use>`) prints a **warning**; convert those to outlines, supply a PNG, or use SVG output (which embeds
-  the logo verbatim and renders everything).
-- **Safety:** SVG logos containing `<script>`, event handlers, `<!ENTITY>`, `<foreignObject>` or external
-  `href`s are refused rather than embedded.
-
-## Symbol layout
-
-Units are ring pitches; angle runs clockwise from "up". The picture, centre outward:
-
-| Zone | Radius (units) | Purpose |
-|---|---|---|
-| Bullseye finder | 0-1.5, 2.5-3.5, 4.5-5.5 ink (plain); two rings at LogoR+1..+2 and +3..+4 (logo versions) | rotation-invariant detection; centre + scale + view geometry |
-| Logo zone | r < LogoR (logo versions only) | clear area for the logo; never sampled |
-| Header rings x3 | 7, 8, 9 (+ shift for logo versions; 32 slots each) | version / mode / length / CRC-16, three rotated copies, majority vote |
-| Data rings | 11 ... 10+K (+ shift) | payload cells; slot count per ring chosen so cells stay ~1.2 units wide |
-| Boundary ring | 12+K (+ shift; solid, 0.8 wide) | fixes scale / shear / perspective |
-| Sync dot | boundary + 1.3, angle 0 | defines slot 0 (orientation) |
-| Quiet zone | 2 units | clear margin |
+Lengths are in *units*; 1 unit is the centre-to-centre pitch of the small data cells. Centre outward:
 
-**Header (32 bits):** `version(4) | mode(4) | length(8) | crc16(16)`.
+| Zone | Purpose |
+|---|---|
+| Logo disc | clear area for the logo; never sampled, so a logo costs no error-correction budget |
+| Inner hexagon | solid hexagonal stroke around the logo disc |
+| **Six petals** | the data: six hex-lattice patches of `3K²+3K+1` cells around the centre patch |
+| Header ring | a hexagonal ring of binary dots/runs: 96 slots = 3 copies of the 32-bit header |
+| Border | a solid hexagonal stroke; its six outer corners locate the symbol |
+
+The petals are the patches of hex-lattice cells within hex distance `K` of `S_i`, where `S_0 = (K+1, K)` in
+axial coordinates and `S_i` is `S_0` rotated by `i·60°`. Because `3K²+3K+1 = (K+1)² + (K+1)K + K²`, those
+vectors generate a sublattice whose fundamental domain is exactly one patch, so the flower tiles with no gaps
+or overlaps (`TestPetalsTile` proves it for every K). The consequence is a slight pinwheel twist: the cell
+lattice is rotated about 26° relative to the hexagonal frame, and the flower is chiral (a mirror image is
+detected and handled).
+
+`--logo-size` picks `K`: small 4, medium 5 (default), large 6, xlarge 7. The logo disc is about 13%, 16%, 18%
+and 19% of the symbol's width.
+
+### Cells, levels and capacity
+
+Each cell holds **1 bit (2 levels: background or ink)** or **2 bits (4 levels: 0, 1/3, 2/3, full ink)**,
+chosen with `--levels auto|2|4`. Levels are Gray-coded so confusing two neighbouring levels flips one bit.
+`auto` (default) uses 2 levels unless the payload needs 4.
+
+Codeword bytes are dealt to the petals round-robin (`byte % 6`), shuffled within each petal, whitened with a
+fixed PRBS and packed into cells. So **one whole petal can be destroyed and the symbol still decodes**
+(checked for every version); the spare capacity of each version is spent on extra Reed-Solomon parity.
+
+| Version | K | Levels | Payload | Reed-Solomon |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | 5 | 2 | 32 B | RS(66,32) |
+| 2 | 6 | 2 | 32 B | RS(90,32) |
+| 3 / 4 | 7 | 2 | 32 / 80 B | RS(126,32) / RS(126,80) |
+| 5 | 4 | 4 | 32 B | RS(90,32) |
+| 6 / 9 | 5 | 4 | 32 / 80 B | RS(132,32) / RS(132,80) |
+| 7 / 10 | 6 | 4 | 32 / 80 B | RS(186,32) / RS(186,80) |
+| 8 / 11 | 7 | 4 | 32 / 80 B | RS(252,32) / RS(252,80) |
+
+The encoder picks the smallest version that fits the chosen `--logo-size`. With 2 levels, small (K=4) has too
+few cells for any payload and 80-byte tags need K=7, so `auto` switches to 4 levels for those.
+
+**Header (32 bits):** `version(4) | mode(4) | length(8) | crc16(16)`, three copies in consecutive blocks of
+the ring, bitwise majority vote (each copy is also tried alone). Modes: 0 raw SHA-256, 1 SHA-256 + tag,
+2-14 reserved (the decoder returns the raw payload, never guesses), 15 escape.
 
-**Modes** (the "mode bit" you asked for, widened to 4 bits so it can grow):
+## Logos in the centre
 
-| Mode | Meaning | Status |
+`--logo <file>` composites the logo into the disc inside the inner hexagon. By default the smallest circle
+enclosing the logo's *visible ink* is scaled to fill 97% of the disc (`--logo-fit viewbox` fits the whole
+image rectangle; `--logo-scale` shrinks or enlarges). `--logo-bg #rrggbb` sets the backdrop colour. SVG output
+embeds an SVG logo as live vector; PNG output with an SVG logo uses a built-in mini rasterizer
+(`internal/svgmini`: filled shapes, paths with curves and arcs, groups, transforms; it **warns** about strokes,
+gradients, text, CSS and `<use>`). SVG logos containing scripts, event handlers, entities, `foreignObject` or
+external hrefs are refused.
+
+## How decoding works (pure Go, `internal/detect`, `internal/decode`)
+
+1. Grayscale, downscale to <=1600 px, adaptive (Bradley) or Otsu threshold, 8-connected components.
+2. For each large component take the convex hull of its outline, reduce it to six vertices, fit a line to each
+   side and intersect neighbours. A component whose hull is a hexagon is a border candidate; the logo and
+   cell ink are never used for detection.
+3. A **homography** from the border's six corners to the canonical hexagon. Because only the outline is used,
+   perspective is handled exactly. All 6 rotations x 2 mirrorings are tried; the header CRC identifies the
+   right one, so no sync mark is needed.
+4. The header ring is read by luminance (ink and paper are measured from the border stroke and the margin),
+   CRC-checked, and the version gives the patch radius.
+5. Every data cell's luminance is sampled and clustered (1-D k-means seeded between ink and paper) into the
+   version's levels. The data is whitened, so all levels are used about equally, which is what makes this
+   blind calibration work without reference swatches.
+6. De-whiten, un-shuffle, Reed-Solomon (Berlekamp-Massey + Chien + Forney).
+7. Any failure falls through to the next threshold, polarity, border candidate, patch radius and orientation.
+
+## Measured robustness (synthetic; `ROSET_ENVELOPE=1 ROSET_ENVELOPE_VER=<n> go test ./internal/decode -run Envelope -v`)
+
+Same geometry (K=5), binary (version 1) versus four levels (version 6):
+
+| Degradation | 2 levels (v1) | 4 levels (v6) |
 |---|---|---|
-| 0 | raw SHA-256 (32 bytes) | implemented |
-| 1 | SHA-256 + tag bytes | implemented |
-| 2-14 | reserved (e.g. algorithm-tagged digests) | decoder returns the raw payload, never guesses |
-| 15 | escape: extended header follows | reserved |
-
-**Versions:** odd versions carry 32 bytes (RS(64,32), corrects 16 bad bytes); even versions carry 80 bytes
-(RS(128,80), corrects 24). 1-2 have the plain bullseye; 3-8 have a logo zone (see above). Data rings are
-chosen as the fewest that hold the codeword. The encoder picks the smallest version that fits.
-
-**Payload pipeline:** zero-pad to capacity -> Reed-Solomon over GF(256) (poly 0x11d) -> split into
-8-cell groups -> deterministic group shuffle (a smudge hits few bytes) -> XOR with a fixed PRBS
-(no big blank or solid areas) -> cells.
-
-## How decoding works (all pure Go, `internal/detect`, `internal/decode`)
-
-1. Grayscale, downscale to <=1600 px, adaptive (Bradley) or Otsu threshold.
-2. 8-connected components; find three nested components sharing a centroid with the bullseye's
-   area ratios (1 : 2.67 : 4.44), or, for logo versions, two nested rings (the logo's own ink is never
-   used for detection, and the boundary ring must also be found).
-3. **Projective frame** from two concentric circles (bullseye ring + boundary ring): the generalized
-   eigenvector of their image conics is the projected centre, the pole-polar relation gives the
-   vanishing line, dividing by it rectifies the view to affine, and an ellipse fit gives the rest.
-   Fallbacks: affine boundary-ring frame, then affine bullseye-only frame.
-4. Find the sync dot's angle, sample every cell in the symbol's own polar frame (5-point vote).
-5. Header CRC -> unshuffle/de-whiten -> Reed-Solomon (Berlekamp-Massey + Chien + Forney).
-6. Any failure falls through to the next thresholding, polarity, frame, slot direction (mirror),
-   finder candidate and version.
-
-## Measured robustness (synthetic, v1 symbol, `RINGSUM_ENVELOPE=1 go test ./internal/decode -run Envelope -v`)
-
-| Degradation | Still decodes up to |
-|---|---|
-| Resolution | ~90 px across (about 2.2 px per unit) |
-| Gaussian blur | sigma 4 px at 500 px width |
-| Sensor-style noise | sigma 90 (of 255) at 500 px |
-| JPEG | quality 4 at 420 px |
-| Occlusion (smudge over data rings) | ~60 degrees of arc; 80 degrees fails |
-| Perspective tilt (pinhole, f = 1.2 x width) | 50 degrees; 60 fails |
-| Rotation / mirror / inverted colours / clutter / off-centre | all pass |
-
-Logo symbols (version 5, the seal above) measure within a hair of that: ~110 px minimum, the same blur, noise,
-JPEG and smudge limits, and tilt up to 40 degrees (50 fails). The logo suite also covers every logo version,
-light and dark themes, a dark backdrop, and adversarial logos (solid black disc, finder-lookalike rings,
-fine checkerboard, full-bleed noise, wide wordmark), alone and under rotation + tilt + blur + noise + JPEG.
-
-**Important caveat:** these are *simulated* distortions of rendered symbols. I have not tested real
-phone photos or printed-and-photographed symbols (no camera in the build environment). Expect real
-lens distortion, glare and motion blur to cost margin; use `--debug-dir` on any failing photo and
-send me `bin.png` / `overlay.png` if you want it tuned.
+| Resolution (symbol width) | 100 px (the smallest tried; ~2.4 px/unit) | 120 px comfortably, 100 px with 22 RS repairs |
+| Gaussian blur, sigma at 500 px width | 4 | 3 (4 fails) |
+| Noise sigma (of 255) at 500 px | 90 | 60 (90 fails) |
+| JPEG quality at 500 px | 4 | 4 |
+| Lighting gradient (darkening across the width) | 65% | 65% (47 of the 50 correctable bytes used) |
+| Whole petals wiped | 1 | 2 |
+| Perspective tilt | 65 degrees (the largest tried) | 65 degrees |
+
+So 2 levels is the most robust, while 4 levels trades blur/noise margin for capacity and extra parity. Both
+cope with rotation, mirroring, inverted colours, clutter, off-centre placement and a rotation + tilt +
+blur + noise + JPEG combination (see `internal/decode/decode_test.go`).
+
+**Caveat:** these are *simulated* distortions of rendered symbols. SVG output was additionally rasterized with
+an independent renderer (`cairosvg`) and decoded. Real phone photos and print-and-photograph runs have not
+been tested; expect lens distortion, glare and motion blur to cost margin. 4 levels in particular depends on
+the camera not clipping or compressing grays, so prefer 2 levels for anything printed.
+Use `--debug-dir` on a failing photo (`bin.png`, `overlay.png`).
 
